# Performance Evaluation

## Findings

### PERF-001: Synchronous File I/O Blocks the Event Loop

- **Rule ID**: PERF-001
- **Severity**: High
- **Category**: I/O Optimization / Concurrency Utilization
- **Location**: Lines 6, 10-11 (`writeFileSync`, `readFileSync`, `existsSync`)

**Description**: All three file system operations — `writeFileSync` (line 6), `existsSync` (line 10), and `readFileSync` (line 10) — are synchronous and will block the event loop for the duration of the disk I/O. For a session management module, these functions are called per-request on the hot path. Under concurrent load, every incoming request that triggers `saveSession` or `loadSession` serializes behind disk I/O, preventing the Node.js event loop from processing any other work (network I/O, timers, other requests).

On a typical server disk, a single `writeFileSync` or `readFileSync` can take 1–10ms. With 100 concurrent requests, this translates to up to ~1 second of cumulative blocking, during which **all** other connections are stalled.

**Evidence**:
```typescript
// Line 6 — blocks event loop during write
writeFileSync(`${SESSION_DIR}/${sessionId}.json`, JSON.stringify(data));

// Lines 10-11 — blocks event loop for stat + read
if (existsSync(path)) return JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, "utf-8"));
```

**Recommendation**: Use the async equivalents from `fs/promises`:

```typescript
import { writeFile, readFile, access } from "fs/promises";
import { constants } from "fs";

const SESSION_DIR = "/tmp/sessions";

async function saveSession(sessionId: string, data: any): Promise<void> {
  await writeFile(`${SESSION_DIR}/${sessionId}.json`, JSON.stringify(data));
}

async function loadSession(sessionId: string): Promise<any> {
  const path = `${SESSION_DIR}/${sessionId}.json`;
  try {
    const content = await readFile(path, "utf-8");
    return JSON.parse(content);
  } catch {
    return null;
  }
}
```

This avoids the `existsSync` + `readFileSync` TOCTOU pattern as a bonus, and unblocks the event loop during all disk operations.

